 when i got  engaged with the disease and since two  years i'm now back here in berlin having  my own group and  doing a kind of hybrid research so we  are using  biochemistry biophysics but also looking  in human brain samples and using mouse  models of the disease to tackle  the questions that we are having and  to come to this your other question how  we basically feed in other branches of  science i think so  since we are operating at the interface  of basic science and translational  science for  therapy development we are feeding  with our results and ideas into drug  development and therapy development  and on the other hand my team and i were  getting  inspiration from all kind of other  science fields  so i think it's important to in order to  develop an interdisciplinary approach  you have to be  always alert about what's going on in  other research so that  manifests for example that i'm currently  reading up on new technologies on  other research fields like cancer  biology at the moment or  genomics and protein biophysics and so  on and so on  what are the technologies which could  push the  next breakthrough forward in this field  if i would know this i would i would get  funding and get all the best people to  work on this but  until then i think um i believe that  actually technology the new technologies  evolving can bring us quite forward  because they open new  pathways and new like views on on the  old problem alzheimer's disease so in  particular i think we need new disease  models  um new disease models going away from  mouse models a bit because  on other animal models because it  becomes quite clear that they are not  really mimicking the human brain  surprisingly or not and i think  new disease models could be all that  that are emerging and important are for  example human derived stem cell systems  such as organoids or so-called  mini-brains that can bring us in an  in vitro dish forward in this and  on the therapy side i think for  therapeutic approaches for inherited  diseases um there's definitely the new  the new com new gene therapy tools that  are developed are very important and  promising to get into human diseases for  inherited diseases and  for spontaneous occurring alzheimer's  disease and other  dementia diseases i think we have to  move towards more holistic combinatorial  diseases not just tackling one  aspect of the disease but the whole  thing  well it seems these kind of diseases are  a very complex  problem what are the key factors for  these diseases did you find new factors  so we found some interesting more  biophysical factors that could  open up a new research area um  what is since we work on molecules what  is  i think also very interesting finding in  the last decade is that a lot of  external factors are triggering dementia  or at least increase the risk for  dementia so  for example it matters what you eat how  much you move how much stress you have  that all influences your brain and your  risk for alzheimer's  and dementia the digestive system as an  example is connected to the brain  through the gut brain axis and with that  your your gut  microbiome so what you have in your  stomach is actually influencing your  brain function  this is a very interesting new field  which is emerging now  we also know now that cardiovascular  system fitness so  is really helping your brain to maintain  for a longer time its function so that  means you have to have good nutrition  you have to move a lot  walk the stairs instead of taking the  elevator and  do not smoke no too much alcohol and no  bad nutrition even pollution air  pollution is making an  impact on your brain and i think with  those  um easy to and to change habits and we  can already um  improve our brain function for the  berlin science week you have  choosed a quite unusual way to  communicate your  topic you are hosting a short theater  play  followed by a discussion why is that  so first one is state it's not really my  idea so i'm just  get sucked into this and i really like  the idea that's why i'm taking part in  this  so the idea for the play dust invite me  for them has no it's by juan clara the  piece  and the idea came from christa muller  who is a  who is a retired actress who was  inspired by the piece when she saw it  and wanted to play it herself  in part to challenge her own aging brain  which i find very interesting  approach for this and the other main  person in this is  um the director of course franz damian  who set that very nicely this  interesting piece  into a living room setting so it becomes  very approachable for people  and the story is about a mother who  loses her memory and then there's this  daughter who has all these like  questions about the past and  yeah i think you should all see it it's  a very very interesting play yeah  it sounds very inspiring thank you so  much susanna wigman for  this interview thanks a lot
 

